Adapted from http://artemisa.sourceforge.net/ (https://sourceforge.net/projects/artemisa/) by Rodrigo do Carmo, Mohamed Nassar and Pablo Masri.

artemisa-nx
***********

artemisa-nx is the Artemisa 1.0.91 release (February 2011, the last release published on
SourceForge) migrated from Python 2 to Python 3. Artemisa needs the PJSUA Python module of
PJSIP. That module was written for Python 2 and PJSIP 1.x; since PJSIP 2.16 (2025) the same
legacy module builds for Python 3, so artemisa-nx is used with PJSIP 2.16 or later
(tested with PJSIP 2.17 and Python 3.11 on Debian 12). Behaviour, configuration files, log
files and reports are unchanged except where listed below. The original README follows.

Changes from the original
=========================

* Python 3 syntax (print, except ... as, configparser, input) and relative imports
  inside the modules package.
* libs/IPy updated from IPy 0.70 (Python 2 only) to IPy 1.01 (same BSD licence, see
  libs/IPy/COPYING).
* nmap, dig, whois and the on_flood/on_spit/on_scanning scripts are run without a
  shell. The original built shell command lines from addresses, host names and ports
  taken from the received SIP message; artemisa-nx passes them as separate arguments
  and does not run nmap, dig or whois for values that are not a plain host name,
  address or port. In actions.conf each word of a parameter template is one argument.
* Comparisons of str with int that Python 3 rejects (results of CheckDNS and CheckPort,
  the recorder and player ids) are written so that they behave as in Python 2.
* When no sound device is found, the PJSUA null audio device is used, so calls are
  still answered with the configured audio file and recorded (the original disabled
  recording). This is what servers and containers without a sound card need.
* When standard input is closed (for example when run as a service), Artemisa keeps
  running instead of exiting; the interactive console works as before on a terminal.
* The process name is set with a bytes argument to prctl (Python 3 ctypes).
* Conference ports are disconnected at the end of a call only if they still exist.
  PJSIP 1.x returned an error for a port that was already gone; PJSIP 2.x built with
  assertions enabled aborts the process (it happened after every unanswered or
  unacknowledged call).
* libs/ip2country (not used by Artemisa) converted to Python 3 syntax.
* Empty logs, results and recorded_calls directories are kept with .gitkeep files.

Prerequisites for artemisa-nx: Python 3, PJSIP 2.16 or later built with CFLAGS=-fPIC and
the legacy PJSUA module installed from pjsip-apps/src/python (python setup.py install),
and optionally nmap, dnsutils (dig) and whois for the active checks.

Artemisa 1.0
************

Documentation
=============

Full documentation is provided inside "doc" directory. Open "/doc/index.html" 
with your browser, or visit http://artemisa.sourceforge.net/documentation/en/

Prerequisites
=============

Artemisa requires the following installed components:

* Python (=> 2.5.5)
* bind-utils (Red Hat, Fedora), bind-tools (Gentoo), dnsutils (Debian)
* PJSIP with the PJSUA Python Module (http://www.pjsip.org/)
* nmap (http://nmap.org/)
* jwhois (http://www.gnu.org/software/jwhois/)

Downloading
===========

Artemisa is free and you can download the latest version from: 
    
    http://artemisa.sourceforge.net/ 

There is also a SVN version for developers: 

    https://artemisa.svn.sourceforge.net/svnroot/artemisa/

Installation
============

Artemisa doesn't require installation. You should unpack the downloaded source 
code and just run it by typing:

    $ ./artemisa.py
    
    or
    
    $ python artemisa.py


DO NOT run Artemisa until having read and set up the configuration files found 
in /conf directory.

